<p>The 2026 World Happiness Report says life satisfaction has fallen sharply over the past decade among people under 25 in some English-speaking countries, and it links heavy social media use to part of that decline. It also draws a clear contrast between online activities: communication, news, learning, and creating content are associated with higher life satisfaction, while social media, gaming, and browsing for fun are associated with lower life evaluations. The global picture includes a brighter note. Finland ranked as the world’s happiest country for the ninth year in a row, with other Nordic countries again near the top of the list. Taken together, the findings suggest that how people use digital technology may matter as much as how much they use it, while also showing that some countries continue to sustain high levels of reported wellbeing.</p><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Teen girls: The report says the effects are especially worrying for teenage girls in English-speaking countries and western Europe.</li><li>Oxford link: The Wellbeing Research Centre at the University of Oxford published the annual report.</li><li>Nordic cluster: Alongside Finland, Iceland, Denmark, and Sweden again ranked among the world’s happiest countries.</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/social-media-makes-people-unhappy-world-happiness-report/a-76422753'>Social media makes people unhappy — World Happiness Report</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/sci-tech/health/world-happiness-report-2026-the-social-media-and-happiness-connect/article70758644.ece'>World Happiness Report 2026: The social media and happiness connect</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/world-happiness-report-highlights-social-medias-negative-impact-131202522'>World Happiness Report highlights social media&#x27;s negative impact</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/article/3347092/worlds-happiest-places-revealed-social-media-weighs-us'>World’s ‘happiest’ places revealed as social media weighs on us</a> - scmp.com</li></ul>